From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: WavJaby <WavJaby@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 23:25:33 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] art: local reference tables below 4 GiB

A local reference is its LrtEntry address with the kind bit set, so on a
64-bit runtime it lies wherever the table was mapped. A 32-bit native
bridge guest keeps a jobject in a 32-bit word: with the table below
4 GiB it holds the runtime own reference unchanged, as on a 32-bit
runtime, and the runtime stays the only owner of local reference
lifetimes. Global and weak global references are table indices and fit
already.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [64-bit runtime hosting a 32-bit guest]
Patch-Reason: the local reference value is the table address; no option,
 allocator hook or launcher setting places the table, and a bridge-side
 reference table would re-implement local frames and DeleteLocalRef.
---
SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
 runtime/jni/local_reference_table.cc | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/runtime/jni/local_reference_table.cc b/runtime/jni/local_reference_table.cc
index 670db19a2259ab463b4ba3f62abe562b0826da5a..5721d154cc1ff1accf239265a8a1dcec4a13e065 100644
--- a/runtime/jni/local_reference_table.cc
+++ b/runtime/jni/local_reference_table.cc
@@ -40,11 +40,13 @@ namespace jni {
 static constexpr bool kDumpStackOnNonLocalReference = false;
 
 // Mmap an "indirect ref table region. Table_bytes is a multiple of a page size.
+// Below 4 GiB: a local reference is the entry's address, and a 32-bit native bridge guest holds
+// it in a 32-bit word.
 static inline MemMap NewLRTMap(size_t table_bytes, std::string* error_msg) {
   return MemMap::MapAnonymous("local ref table",
                               table_bytes,
                               PROT_READ | PROT_WRITE,
-                              /*low_4gb=*/ false,
+                              /*low_4gb=*/ true,
                               error_msg);
 }
 
